 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T20:24+08:00",
 "answer": "**For a short trip outside, probably not**—Singapore’s MOH says an N95 isn’t needed for a normal commute. But haze is currently affecting the air: the latest official update, at **7 pm on October 17**, reported a 24-hour PSI of **130–199 (Unhealthy)** and 1-hour PM2.5 readings ranging from **Elevated to High**. ([weather.gov.sg](https://www.weather.gov.sg/haze-situation-update-17-october-2026/))\n\nIf you’ll be outdoors for a long time, especially exercising, cut back on exertion; a **well-fitting N95, KN95, or equivalent** can reduce exposure. Be more cautious if you’re pregnant, older, a child, or have heart or lung disease. Readings fluctuate, so check your area’s latest 1-hour PM2.5 in the myENV app or haze.gov.sg before heading out. ([moh.gov.sg](https://www.moh.gov.sg/others/haze/?utm_source=openai))",
